WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.097 --> 00:00:03.246
Wren: That gun is my favorite
part of the shot though.

00:00:03.246 --> 00:00:04.497
Clint: Dude, it's so frickin' cool.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:07.404
Wren: It's like, that is
good design right there.

00:00:07.404 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi, everybody. My name's Niko.

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:10.108
Hey, and I'm Wren.

00:00:10.108 --> 00:00:10.941
And I'm Clint.

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:12.929
Niko: And we do a show on the
Corridor Crew YouTube channel

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.414
called "VFX Artists React,"

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.420
where we break down some of the coolest

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.487
and perhaps some of the
worst visual effects shots

00:00:18.487 --> 00:00:19.573
that are done in movies, TV,

00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:21.851
and all that good media that you consume.

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:23.114
Today, we're gonna be breaking down

00:00:23.114 --> 00:00:24.649
some of the best and worst

00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:27.162
visual effects fight scenes for Insider.

00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:28.528
Clint: Let's jump in.

00:00:32.586 --> 00:00:34.159
Niko: There is a taste of
one of the signature effects

00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:35.457
of "Blade II" here.

00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:37.265
Not the vampire disintegration,

00:00:37.265 --> 00:00:38.433
but the digital doubles.

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:39.391
Wren: That was what I was noticing.

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:41.224
It was cleanly going back
and forth between, like,

00:00:41.224 --> 00:00:44.834
actual, real people fighting
on set to a digi-double,

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:46.760
but I didn't particularly
see the transition.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.524
Oh, it's 'cause there wasn't a transition.

00:00:49.524 --> 00:00:51.315
Clint: No, no, it's a new
-- it's just a new shot.

00:00:51.315 --> 00:00:53.587
Wren: And a digi-double
jumps off of his back.

00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:55.667
And you can notice he doesn't bend at all.

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:57.739
Niko: [laughs] He has no
reaction from him on it.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:00:59.072
Clint: I get what they're going for.

00:00:59.072 --> 00:01:00.820
It's like these ninjas are --

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.813
well, they're vampire ninjas, for one.

00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:04.126
They're superhuman,

00:01:04.126 --> 00:01:05.735
and they're light as a feather,

00:01:05.735 --> 00:01:07.238
and they're silent as night.

00:01:07.238 --> 00:01:08.671
But still, like,

00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:10.893
when you got this frickin'
Gumby-looking effect

00:01:10.893 --> 00:01:12.494
coming off his shoulder....

00:01:12.494 --> 00:01:13.452
Wren: Real quick.

00:01:13.452 --> 00:01:15.419
What do you mean by "Gumby effects"?

00:01:15.419 --> 00:01:17.043
Clint: It's just this, like, lanky little

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:19.252
amorphous little eraser thing.

00:01:19.252 --> 00:01:20.239
[all laughing]

00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:21.072
I don't know.

00:01:21.072 --> 00:01:22.888
Like, they're flying through the air

00:01:22.888 --> 00:01:25.886
and it's like there's no
sense of gravity or weight.

00:01:25.886 --> 00:01:27.526
Wren: Once she is in the air,

00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:29.740
I feel like she's falling at
the correct gravitational rate.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.698
Niko: I agree, yeah.

00:01:30.698 --> 00:01:32.767
Wren: So I think it
actually is pretty good

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.014
as far as the animation
of her flight path.

00:01:35.014 --> 00:01:36.040
In this next shot here,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.533
Wesley Snipes jumps off the cabinet,

00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:38.455
but at the very beginning,

00:01:38.455 --> 00:01:40.073
that's not a real Wesley Snipes there,

00:01:40.073 --> 00:01:41.039
and he immediately jumps off.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:43.582
And you can kind of see how
he has a very quick motion.

00:01:43.582 --> 00:01:44.499
It's a little inhuman.

00:01:44.499 --> 00:01:45.852
He can't accelerate that fast

00:01:45.852 --> 00:01:47.250
just off of the force of one leg.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.433
Although, they did get the
motion of the cabinet good.

00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:51.187
Someone probably yanked it, yeah.

00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:53.116
Clint: You wanna have as
much real-world reaction

00:01:53.116 --> 00:01:54.450
to your CG stuff as possible,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.696
and this is a really good example of that.

00:01:55.696 --> 00:01:57.004
Wren: They're getting the contact shadows

00:01:57.004 --> 00:01:58.543
and the reflections really well

00:01:58.543 --> 00:01:59.947
between the CG characters and the ground.

00:01:59.947 --> 00:02:01.623
And they have the perfect reference,

00:02:01.623 --> 00:02:03.726
because they actually
shoot the scene for real

00:02:03.726 --> 00:02:04.891
in some of these shots.

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:05.932
Clint: Yeah, and especially when you have

00:02:05.932 --> 00:02:07.341
your main actor, Wesley,

00:02:07.341 --> 00:02:09.553
who is actually a legit martial artist.

00:02:09.553 --> 00:02:11.442
He's actually really good at martial arts.

00:02:11.442 --> 00:02:12.307
So you can see he's able

00:02:12.307 --> 00:02:13.752
to pull off some of these moves, you know.

00:02:13.752 --> 00:02:15.700
Wren: There was a shot here
that really caught my eye.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.535
It was when they were
both against the backlight

00:02:17.535 --> 00:02:19.141
and they're just kind of
jumping over each other.

00:02:19.141 --> 00:02:20.025
Clint: Yeah, yeah, yeah. Right here.

00:02:20.025 --> 00:02:21.081
Wren: That, yeah.
Clint: Here we go.

00:02:21.081 --> 00:02:23.674
They're trying to cram
too many spins into this.

00:02:23.674 --> 00:02:24.800
Just the one too many.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.787
Niko: This second shot, in my opinion,

00:02:25.787 --> 00:02:28.851
is the least realistic shot, right here.

00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:30.915
They move like they're in a Pixar film.

00:02:30.915 --> 00:02:31.748
Clint: Agreed.

00:02:31.748 --> 00:02:33.218
Niko: Especially his little
pop-up at the end there.

00:02:33.218 --> 00:02:35.398
Clint: It's the Gumby.
It's the whip, zip, whip.

00:02:35.398 --> 00:02:36.671
It's, like, 15% stylized,

00:02:36.671 --> 00:02:38.360
like 15% too much sauce.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.469
Niko: To this day, the
greatest challenge right now

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:41.117
is animation, in my opinion.
Clint: Agreed.

00:02:41.117 --> 00:02:42.895
Wren: Basically, they're using
what are called key frames

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.285
to hand-animate,

00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:46.280
like you would, like,
a puppet or whatever.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.772
They're doing that here, but digitally.

00:02:47.772 --> 00:02:49.453
Niko: So, if you had to
do this shot again today,

00:02:49.453 --> 00:02:51.724
you might use a little
bit more motion capture.

00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:54.374
But, end of the day, an
artist still has to go in

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.921
and generally tweak things by hand.

00:02:55.921 --> 00:02:58.569
Wren: Like, it's literally the
exact same process back then

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:01.020
as it is today for something like this.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.287
Niko: There's a bit of historical context

00:03:02.287 --> 00:03:03.858
that's actually super important here.

00:03:03.858 --> 00:03:05.590
This is 2002.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.693
"The Matrix" came out in 1999,

00:03:07.693 --> 00:03:09.728
and we are still hot on the tails

00:03:09.728 --> 00:03:12.168
of, like, "Matrix" fever.

00:03:12.168 --> 00:03:13.680
Everybody's doing bullet time.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.950
Everybody's doing slow motion.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.799
And with "Blade II," they were like,

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:19.327
"We have something that's
even better than bullet time."

00:03:19.327 --> 00:03:20.464
And that was their digital doubles.

00:03:20.464 --> 00:03:21.403
They're stretching it out,

00:03:21.403 --> 00:03:24.084
but for the time, it's pretty dang good.

00:03:24.084 --> 00:03:25.075
Wren: This is far better than in

00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:26.100
"Captain America: Civil War"

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.252
when Black Panther does that
little, like, spinning kick,

00:03:28.252 --> 00:03:29.745
and he's like, [mimics sound of kick]

00:03:29.745 --> 00:03:31.737
and then he quickly falls to the ground.

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:34.436
[weapon whooshing]

00:03:34.436 --> 00:03:36.167
[T'Challa shouting]

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.640
Clint: That is buff Jesse Eisenberg!

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:40.897
Niko: We're gonna try to pinpoint

00:03:40.897 --> 00:03:41.957
every time there's a hidden cut

00:03:41.957 --> 00:03:43.737
in this one-take fight scene.

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:46.353
Clint: Starting with the gunshot.

00:03:46.353 --> 00:03:47.186
Wren: This is one shot,

00:03:47.186 --> 00:03:48.493
and we're transitioning
to a new shot here.

00:03:48.493 --> 00:03:50.124
Niko: One. That was a
transition, in my opinion.

00:03:50.124 --> 00:03:53.103
Yeah. That's probably a CG railing or,

00:03:53.103 --> 00:03:54.699
you know, banister right there

00:03:54.699 --> 00:03:55.760
that we've moved through.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.429
The camera would physically
have to move through it.

00:03:57.429 --> 00:03:58.587
Wren: This dude here's a digi-double.

00:03:58.587 --> 00:03:59.779
Niko: I don't think it's a digital double.

00:03:59.779 --> 00:04:01.285
I think it's retimed performance.

00:04:01.285 --> 00:04:03.393
Wren: Oh, you know
what? That's exactly it.

00:04:03.393 --> 00:04:06.170
Clint: So, we actually
had the stunt performer

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.287
who played "Black Panther"
come to our studio

00:04:09.287 --> 00:04:11.647
and give us a little
lowdown on how to fall.

00:04:11.647 --> 00:04:12.885
One of the things he said was

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.975
you wanna start in the ready position

00:04:14.975 --> 00:04:17.199
with your knees bent,
hunched over, ready to jump

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:18.702
so you're not standing up
straight, and then you go,

00:04:18.702 --> 00:04:20.053
"All right, here we go." [grunts]

00:04:20.053 --> 00:04:21.330
You're already ready to go.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:22.547
Let's see if he's in the ready position.

00:04:22.547 --> 00:04:24.424
Niko: Almost, almost.
Clint: Kind of, kind of. Yeah.

00:04:24.424 --> 00:04:26.486
So, maybe he wasn't ready enough

00:04:26.486 --> 00:04:28.093
to, like, go right into it

00:04:28.093 --> 00:04:29.172
and he had to get that wind-up,

00:04:29.172 --> 00:04:30.365
which they were trying to cut out.

00:04:30.365 --> 00:04:31.915
Niko: Get ready for No. 2.

00:04:31.915 --> 00:04:32.748
Clint: Yeah, here it comes.

00:04:32.748 --> 00:04:34.441
Masked by the banister.

00:04:34.441 --> 00:04:35.399
Niko: Oh, right there, two.

00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:36.605
And then...three,

00:04:36.605 --> 00:04:39.336
right there by the by
the extras, with a wipe.

00:04:39.336 --> 00:04:41.538
And boom. Wipe. Four.

00:04:41.538 --> 00:04:42.371
Five.

00:04:42.371 --> 00:04:43.561
Wren: Yeah.
Clint: With the banister.

00:04:43.561 --> 00:04:44.713
That is Andy Serkis, isn't it?

00:04:44.713 --> 00:04:45.760
Wren: Yeah, it is.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.472
We got Gollum and we got Bilbo in here.

00:04:47.472 --> 00:04:50.613
[arm blasts]
[T'Challa shouts]

00:04:50.613 --> 00:04:52.606
Wren: OK. I think that was
another transition right there,

00:04:52.606 --> 00:04:55.295
because that was, like, a
full effects shot there.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.783
Niko: You know, he could be
picking up a real thing then.

00:04:56.783 --> 00:04:59.157
And then the moment it
explodes, there's a cut there,

00:04:59.157 --> 00:05:02.101
and you're going to a CG
cabinet or whatever exploding.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:03.941
You're going to a CG Chadwick Boseman

00:05:03.941 --> 00:05:05.381
flying back through the railing.

00:05:05.381 --> 00:05:06.386
I think it's a real guy
going through the railing.

00:05:06.386 --> 00:05:08.588
Wren: Well, there's at
least a CG version of him

00:05:08.588 --> 00:05:09.772
flying through the air.

00:05:09.772 --> 00:05:11.668
And maybe it's a stunt
right at the last moment,

00:05:11.668 --> 00:05:13.867
when he actually hits the thing.

00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:15.187
What do you think of the debris?

00:05:15.187 --> 00:05:18.344
Clint: I think it looks fine at first,

00:05:18.344 --> 00:05:20.357
but look at the paper by his hand.

00:05:20.357 --> 00:05:21.711
Look at it by his hand, right there.

00:05:21.711 --> 00:05:24.052
Niko: Yes, I agree. The
paper's a little weird.

00:05:24.052 --> 00:05:25.655
Wren: I don't see enough
of them, like, spinning.

00:05:25.655 --> 00:05:26.488
Clint: "Rush Hour 2."

00:05:26.488 --> 00:05:28.096
Niko: With all, like, the
money in the air or something?

00:05:28.096 --> 00:05:28.933
Clint: Yeah, yeah, yeah,
the [mimics action].

00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:31.168
Oh, yeah, yeah. OK, here's
the reference, "Rush Hour 1."

00:05:31.168 --> 00:05:32.371
Wren: That's realistic-looking paper.

00:05:32.371 --> 00:05:34.263
Niko: That's how it happens
when money's in the air.

00:05:34.263 --> 00:05:36.178
Very spinny, very flickery.

00:05:36.178 --> 00:05:37.808
Wren: I feel like I wanna see more dust.

00:05:37.808 --> 00:05:41.391
There is no dust in this
explosive shot at all.

00:05:41.391 --> 00:05:43.234
Like, normally, when something explodes,

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.653
yeah, you get all the big
pieces of debris flying away,

00:05:45.653 --> 00:05:47.773
but you also get a lot
of microparticulates.

00:05:47.773 --> 00:05:49.970
But the problem is that all of that dust

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.210
would obscure the background

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.717
and the stunt of him hitting the ground.

00:05:53.717 --> 00:05:55.654
So I think it was a very
intentional decision

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:57.236
to not put that dust in there,

00:05:57.236 --> 00:05:59.808
so that you can actually
see through the explosion

00:05:59.808 --> 00:06:01.490
and him hitting the pool table.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.127
Clint: Dang, dude, you're
a frickin' mad scientist.

00:06:03.127 --> 00:06:05.754
I think you're exactly right.
That makes so much sense.

00:06:05.754 --> 00:06:07.547
Niko: I mean, you can see
the dust from the pool table

00:06:07.547 --> 00:06:08.739
when that breaks, for example.

00:06:08.739 --> 00:06:10.481
Clint: Yeah, exactly.
Which looks really nice.

00:06:10.481 --> 00:06:11.347
[Niko mimics explosion]

00:06:12.206 --> 00:06:14.492
Klaue: You look just like your old man.

00:06:14.492 --> 00:06:16.015
Wren: That gun is my favorite
part of the shot, though.

00:06:16.015 --> 00:06:17.153
Clint: Dude, it's so frickin' cool.

00:06:17.153 --> 00:06:18.785
Wren: It's like, that is
good design right there.

00:06:18.785 --> 00:06:20.454
For that effect to have to happen,

00:06:20.454 --> 00:06:23.448
they have to literally paint out his arm.

00:06:23.448 --> 00:06:26.203
If his arm is blocking,
you know, the camera

00:06:26.203 --> 00:06:27.241
and you wanna remove the arm,

00:06:27.241 --> 00:06:29.562
now you're having to
fill back in the face.

00:06:29.562 --> 00:06:30.988
Niko: So all the tight pattern work

00:06:30.988 --> 00:06:32.984
on the window behind his arm,

00:06:32.984 --> 00:06:34.528
when they're putting the patches back in

00:06:34.528 --> 00:06:36.152
to cover up the spots where
they're erasing his arm,

00:06:36.152 --> 00:06:38.237
they're having to match all those lines

00:06:38.237 --> 00:06:40.385
and the perspective and the motion blur.

00:06:40.385 --> 00:06:42.026
Clint: There's one other
thing I wanna bring up.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:43.341
Go back to the beginning of the one-take.

00:06:43.341 --> 00:06:44.874
All right, Niko. All right, Wren.

00:06:44.874 --> 00:06:46.341
I want your harshest opinions.

00:06:46.341 --> 00:06:47.752
Right there, done. Stop.
Niko: Those muzzle flashes?

00:06:47.752 --> 00:06:48.585
Clint: Yeah, go back.

00:06:48.585 --> 00:06:49.510
Wren: Oh, we're looking
at the muzzle flashes?

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.343
Niko: Yeah. Oh, man.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:52.319
You know, how can a movie this big

00:06:52.319 --> 00:06:54.475
have the jankiest muzzle flashes still?

00:06:54.475 --> 00:06:56.170
Clint: By the way, guys,
just to let you know,

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:58.186
Niko is the muzzle-flash master.

00:06:58.186 --> 00:06:59.644
Niko: What is smoke?

00:06:59.644 --> 00:07:00.822
Well, what is fire?

00:07:00.822 --> 00:07:03.547
In fact, fire and smoke
are the same particles.

00:07:03.547 --> 00:07:05.625
Fire is those particles
when they're heated up

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.996
and emitting photons 'cause they're hot.

00:07:07.996 --> 00:07:10.776
And then they cool, and it turns to smoke.

00:07:10.776 --> 00:07:11.619
Well, what do we see here?

00:07:11.619 --> 00:07:13.123
Wren: I see fire and smoke!

00:07:13.123 --> 00:07:15.052
Niko: I see fire and
smoke at the same time!

00:07:15.052 --> 00:07:16.174
That's impossible!

00:07:16.174 --> 00:07:17.066
So, here's the other thing.

00:07:17.066 --> 00:07:18.767
All right, in one frame,

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:21.054
these particles of burning gunpowder

00:07:21.054 --> 00:07:24.661
have gone from the chamber
where the bullet was

00:07:24.661 --> 00:07:26.390
all the way to about,
maybe, eight inches out

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:28.073
in front of the gun. So in one frame,

00:07:28.073 --> 00:07:30.238
we've gone maybe 16 inches.

00:07:30.238 --> 00:07:32.465
If we're maintaining the same
velocity, in the next frame,

00:07:32.465 --> 00:07:34.501
it should be 32 inches out
from the front of the gun.

00:07:34.501 --> 00:07:36.551
Oh, but we just advanced
another two inches,

00:07:36.551 --> 00:07:38.176
at most, with the smoke.

00:07:38.176 --> 00:07:39.486
So somehow the smoke is moving

00:07:39.486 --> 00:07:42.553
at the speed of, like, a slow punch.

00:07:42.553 --> 00:07:44.983
You just get the weakest
punch coming out of the gun.

00:07:44.983 --> 00:07:46.477
Clint: You know, it looks
like, the smoke looks like

00:07:46.477 --> 00:07:48.901
those comped-in cold-breath effects.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:51.216
Wren: That being said,
this movie is notorious

00:07:51.216 --> 00:07:53.545
for having a really tight timeline

00:07:53.545 --> 00:07:55.035
to do all the effects.

00:07:55.035 --> 00:07:56.028
Clint: We're going in harsh, right?

00:07:56.028 --> 00:07:57.369
We're going in harsh. We're explaining,

00:07:57.369 --> 00:07:58.335
"Oh, this should look like this,

00:07:58.335 --> 00:07:59.168
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah."

00:07:59.168 --> 00:08:00.185
But, like, the artists,

00:08:00.185 --> 00:08:01.971
they probably know all that already.

00:08:01.971 --> 00:08:03.455
And they're doing the best they can

00:08:03.455 --> 00:08:04.488
with the time they have.

00:08:04.488 --> 00:08:06.605
So, Niko, why wouldn't they

00:08:06.605 --> 00:08:08.129
have just done the gun practically?

00:08:08.129 --> 00:08:09.407
Niko: So, that's a really good question.

00:08:09.407 --> 00:08:11.073
And, you know, it totally makes sense.

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:13.240
Why don't they use blank-firing
guns on a set like this?

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:14.574
It's a big movie, can't they afford it?

00:08:14.574 --> 00:08:15.873
I've filmed with blanks before.

00:08:15.873 --> 00:08:17.615
The thing about blank-firing guns

00:08:17.615 --> 00:08:20.164
is they are actually still real guns,

00:08:20.164 --> 00:08:22.457
and they still make a lot of noise.

00:08:22.457 --> 00:08:24.788
So when you are doing blanks
on set -- look at this set.

00:08:24.788 --> 00:08:27.208
You have a bunch of extras in this shot.

00:08:27.208 --> 00:08:28.754
So every single person there

00:08:28.754 --> 00:08:31.754
would have to have earplugs
on or ear protection on.

00:08:31.754 --> 00:08:34.772
Potentially, some people are
having to wear eye protection.

00:08:34.772 --> 00:08:37.636
Plus, you still have a zone in front of

00:08:37.636 --> 00:08:39.490
a gun that shoots a blank that's deadly.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.731
Wren: That still has energy,
and that energy can kill you.

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:45.512
Klaue: I made it rain! [laughs]

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:50.734
Wren: We're gonna look
at the chat-ee-uh fight.

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:53.635
Clint: The chat-ee-uh? It's
pronounced "cha-too-ya-who."

00:08:53.635 --> 00:08:56.137
Niko: [laughs] Château fight.

00:08:56.137 --> 00:08:59.451
[mimicking action soundtrack]

00:08:59.451 --> 00:09:01.365
Wren: "Matrix" fights have
a certain aesthetic to them.

00:09:01.365 --> 00:09:03.393
It's all about the ballet dance, you know?

00:09:03.393 --> 00:09:04.689
It's not about, like, the actual

00:09:04.689 --> 00:09:06.668
fighting mechanics of, like....

00:09:06.668 --> 00:09:08.847
Niko: There's a lot of,
like, arms bent, elbows back,

00:09:08.847 --> 00:09:11.293
and, like, punching in from the sides.

00:09:11.293 --> 00:09:12.326
Especially in the first "Matrix,"

00:09:12.326 --> 00:09:14.810
every fight starts with bop, bop, bop.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:15.874
And then it goes from there.

00:09:15.874 --> 00:09:17.541
[Clint mimics punch sounds]

00:09:17.541 --> 00:09:19.673
[firearms banging]

00:09:19.673 --> 00:09:21.850
Wren: This is an example of
firing blanks, then, right?

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:22.683
Niko: Yeah.
Wren: Yeah.

00:09:22.683 --> 00:09:23.722
Clint: Look at the guy's face.

00:09:23.722 --> 00:09:25.298
He's like, [mimics firearms].

00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:26.629
Into his chest and bicep,

00:09:26.629 --> 00:09:30.703
you can see the actual energy
of the blank-firing gun.

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:32.954
Wren: Are they wearing ear protection?

00:09:32.954 --> 00:09:34.189
Niko: Oh, yeah. Definitely.

00:09:34.189 --> 00:09:35.105
Wren: You can't see it.

00:09:35.105 --> 00:09:36.621
Clint: Roto'd out? Or....

00:09:36.621 --> 00:09:37.679
Niko: It's probably --
Clint: No, you can see it.

00:09:37.679 --> 00:09:39.315
Niko: It's flesh-colored
earplugs, most likely.

00:09:39.315 --> 00:09:41.391
Wren: So, but, like, those
bullet casings flying out too,

00:09:41.391 --> 00:09:42.349
those are real?
Niko: Mm-hmm.

00:09:42.349 --> 00:09:43.709
Clint: Yeah.
Niko: Definitely. They're hot.

00:09:43.709 --> 00:09:45.510
Wren: So, the bullets are obviously CG.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.942
Wait. But then that shot of the bullets

00:09:46.942 --> 00:09:48.653
hitting the ground is real. You think?

00:09:48.653 --> 00:09:49.505
Niko: Oh, yeah. Definitely.

00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:50.725
Wren: Yeah, yeah. That's real.

00:09:50.725 --> 00:09:52.278
The thing about all of
this fight here, though,

00:09:52.278 --> 00:09:54.438
is that it's all real.
Clint: Yep.

00:09:54.438 --> 00:09:56.179
Wren: None of this is CG.

00:09:56.179 --> 00:09:59.638
However, that doesn't mean there
are no visual effects here.

00:09:59.638 --> 00:10:01.174
Do you know why that is?

00:10:01.174 --> 00:10:02.774
Niko: Because it's a movie? [laughs]

00:10:02.774 --> 00:10:04.671
Wren: No, it's wirework!

00:10:04.671 --> 00:10:06.373
There are tons of wires

00:10:06.373 --> 00:10:08.237
suspending them from the ceiling,

00:10:08.237 --> 00:10:09.976
so that they're able to
actually float around.

00:10:09.976 --> 00:10:11.234
And the effects work is going into

00:10:11.234 --> 00:10:13.634
removing all of those wires.

00:10:13.634 --> 00:10:15.756
Clint: Oh, that's so sick.

00:10:15.756 --> 00:10:17.235
Wren: God, that sword's,
like, actually there.

00:10:17.235 --> 00:10:19.209
Like, probably stuck to
some sort of chest piece

00:10:19.209 --> 00:10:20.104
under his clothing.

00:10:20.104 --> 00:10:21.392
That's my favorite shot!

00:10:21.392 --> 00:10:23.637
The way his legs come up as he's diving

00:10:23.637 --> 00:10:25.233
off of the thing with a sword, like, ugh.

00:10:25.233 --> 00:10:26.582
Niko: Is it hard to paint out wires?

00:10:26.582 --> 00:10:27.985
Wren: It can be easy if you have, like,

00:10:27.985 --> 00:10:29.864
a really uniform background.

00:10:29.864 --> 00:10:31.110
But if you have a complex background,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.737
especially if you have a
person behind the wires,

00:10:33.737 --> 00:10:35.549
it can become a bit of a process.

00:10:35.549 --> 00:10:38.165
I mean, they probably had a
pretty decent-sized team here.

00:10:38.165 --> 00:10:40.150
At least probably five or six people

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:41.675
just doing wire removal on this.

00:10:41.675 --> 00:10:42.510
Clint: For sure.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.177
Niko: You can see the
wires in another shot

00:10:44.177 --> 00:10:45.703
from the first "Matrix,"

00:10:45.703 --> 00:10:47.789
when he does the backflip
away from the train

00:10:47.789 --> 00:10:50.420
and he goes, "My name...is Neo."

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.461
Wren: So, I guess this is a
type of dagger called a sai.

00:10:57.461 --> 00:10:59.029
How did they have them fly off the wall

00:10:59.029 --> 00:11:00.498
and into his hands?

00:11:00.498 --> 00:11:02.460
At first, that first shot
there, I was like, oh,

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:03.941
they probably just had the dagger on there

00:11:03.941 --> 00:11:04.965
with, like, some fishing line

00:11:04.965 --> 00:11:06.605
and just tugged it off the wall.

00:11:06.605 --> 00:11:08.610
Niko: It's definitely CG. Look
at the way it's comped in,

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.831
the way the motion blur works.

00:11:10.831 --> 00:11:12.935
The way it's shiny and
picking up light as it moves.

00:11:12.935 --> 00:11:14.907
Clint: The dim highlight streaks?

00:11:14.907 --> 00:11:18.094
Niko: Yeah. And you can see
a pop in the background.

00:11:18.094 --> 00:11:19.420
Wren: Yeah. So the first shot there,

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:21.065
on the left, it probably started,

00:11:21.065 --> 00:11:22.579
they probably actually had a real dagger

00:11:22.579 --> 00:11:23.808
hung up on the wall,

00:11:23.808 --> 00:11:24.929
and then they cut to the clean plate

00:11:24.929 --> 00:11:26.271
where the dagger is not there

00:11:26.271 --> 00:11:29.623
and a CG dagger has replaced
it, flying through the air.

00:11:29.623 --> 00:11:32.185
So, that's nice because it's
a good transition point.

00:11:32.185 --> 00:11:33.814
It's an impact point
that you can transition

00:11:33.814 --> 00:11:35.838
from the real blade to the CG blade,

00:11:35.838 --> 00:11:37.184
because now it's in motion.

00:11:37.184 --> 00:11:39.152
And the first time we see the CG blade,

00:11:39.152 --> 00:11:41.190
it's got full motion blur going on.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:42.829
Clint: It's masked by the motion blur.

00:11:42.829 --> 00:11:45.103
Wren: Yeah. I bet he's
actually spinning those blades.

00:11:45.103 --> 00:11:47.286
That's a move that a real person can do.

00:11:47.286 --> 00:11:48.458
Niko: Also, if you watch it slowly,

00:11:48.458 --> 00:11:49.632
the only really complicated thing's

00:11:49.632 --> 00:11:51.054
the little loop on his fingers.

00:11:51.054 --> 00:11:55.389
He literally just takes
them and goes, huh, huh.

00:11:55.389 --> 00:11:57.485
Holds them out and brings them in.

00:11:57.485 --> 00:11:59.385
Yeah, there's no CG doubles here.

00:11:59.385 --> 00:12:01.018
There's no, like, CG destruction.

00:12:01.018 --> 00:12:02.407
You know, the wires being painted out,

00:12:02.407 --> 00:12:03.449
and that's basically it.

00:12:03.449 --> 00:12:04.282
Clint: And the bullets.

00:12:04.282 --> 00:12:05.635
Niko: And the bullets, of course.

00:12:05.635 --> 00:12:07.426
Wren: You know, it's
funny, 'cause it's like,

00:12:07.426 --> 00:12:09.809
there's no CG doubles in this scene,

00:12:09.809 --> 00:12:11.039
but this is also the same movie

00:12:11.039 --> 00:12:12.590
that has the burly brawl.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.992
There is a whole sequence where, like,

00:12:14.992 --> 00:12:18.108
literally 100 digital
characters are all fighting.

00:12:19.116 --> 00:12:21.449
Merovingian: OK. You have some skill.

00:12:23.129 --> 00:12:26.296
[explosion whooshing]

00:12:28.736 --> 00:12:29.569
Niko: This is the closest thing

00:12:29.569 --> 00:12:30.989
to a "Dragon Ball Z" fight in real life.

00:12:30.989 --> 00:12:32.351
Wren: Yeah, that was exactly
what I was gonna say.

00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:33.467
That's why I dug this so much.

00:12:33.467 --> 00:12:35.370
This is like -- this
isn't a superhero film.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.131
This is a "Dragon Ball Z" film.

00:12:37.131 --> 00:12:37.964
Clint: Yeah, exactly.

00:12:37.964 --> 00:12:39.926
Wren: 'Cause it's like, "Man
of Steel" kind of did this

00:12:39.926 --> 00:12:41.973
with, like, Zod, I think was his name.

00:12:41.973 --> 00:12:43.267
But I feel like this one

00:12:43.267 --> 00:12:45.100
had the style to back it up.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.638
Niko: You might think that
they're doing CG doubles

00:12:46.638 --> 00:12:48.954
for a lot of this, but they actually put

00:12:48.954 --> 00:12:51.258
both Keanu Reeves and Hugo Weaving

00:12:51.258 --> 00:12:52.877
on something called a tuning fork,

00:12:52.877 --> 00:12:55.149
which is this long pole

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:56.624
and then hooked up to a
little thing on their waist

00:12:56.624 --> 00:12:58.362
that they could just spin them around on.

00:12:58.362 --> 00:12:59.666
Clint: Can you imagine
friggin' Hugo Weaving

00:12:59.666 --> 00:13:01.858
attached to this thing for days on end?

00:13:01.858 --> 00:13:03.195
Just like, "Oh, boy."

00:13:03.195 --> 00:13:05.306
[all laugh]

00:13:05.306 --> 00:13:07.110
Wren: Like, "I didn't have to
do this when I was Elrond!"

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.549
Niko: So, a lot of this stuff
is tuning-fork performance,

00:13:09.549 --> 00:13:11.514
then there are some CG-double shots.

00:13:11.514 --> 00:13:12.737
So, like, that was probably a tuning fork.

00:13:12.737 --> 00:13:13.867
And then here, whoop!

00:13:13.867 --> 00:13:15.272
Wren: Yeah, CG.
Niko: CG.

00:13:15.272 --> 00:13:18.245
And you can just tell from the animation.

00:13:18.245 --> 00:13:20.001
[mimicking explosion]

00:13:20.001 --> 00:13:21.515
This is damn good CG

00:13:21.515 --> 00:13:23.033
for the time this came out.
Wren: Yeah. Oh, my God.

00:13:23.033 --> 00:13:24.590
And now they're gonna have a five-minute

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.801
philosophical conversation.

00:13:26.801 --> 00:13:28.159
Clint: Oh, here it comes.
Wren: There it is.

00:13:28.159 --> 00:13:30.543
[Clint mimics action]

00:13:30.543 --> 00:13:32.131
So, the first thing to note here is that

00:13:32.131 --> 00:13:34.539
from beginning to end,
the entirety of this shot

00:13:34.539 --> 00:13:35.651
is computer-generated.

00:13:35.651 --> 00:13:37.617
There is no plate photography

00:13:37.617 --> 00:13:39.657
from the moment this shot starts.

00:13:39.657 --> 00:13:42.969
Both Agent Smith and Neo are full CG.

00:13:42.969 --> 00:13:44.713
All the water-simulation stuff going on,

00:13:44.713 --> 00:13:48.204
his face, the skin
morphing around the punch,

00:13:48.204 --> 00:13:50.464
fist, the imprints left on,

00:13:50.464 --> 00:13:52.155
even the discoloration of his skin.

00:13:52.155 --> 00:13:53.398
Niko: But when you're like, "OK,

00:13:53.398 --> 00:13:54.992
I need to get Hugo Weaving's face

00:13:54.992 --> 00:13:56.949
to deform after it gets punched."

00:13:56.949 --> 00:13:58.697
It's not like Adobe's like,
"Well, here's the plug-in

00:13:58.697 --> 00:14:00.789
for Hugo Weaving face deformations."

00:14:00.789 --> 00:14:02.460
[all laughing]

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.810
That stuff doesn't exist, so --

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:04.971
Wren: Come on, Adobe! [chuckles]

00:14:04.971 --> 00:14:07.413
Niko: You know, coders and
software engineers and artists

00:14:07.413 --> 00:14:10.871
have to get together and
basically create the tools

00:14:10.871 --> 00:14:13.741
to just be able to do a
shot like this from scratch.

00:14:13.741 --> 00:14:15.760
It's like going to make a car,

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:18.109
but you start off with,
like, raw iron ore,

00:14:18.109 --> 00:14:19.978
and now you need to turn
it into metal somehow.

00:14:19.978 --> 00:14:21.823
Wren: This is such a hero shot, too.

00:14:21.823 --> 00:14:23.288
Like, I don't think we've seen

00:14:23.288 --> 00:14:25.069
a digi-double this close up

00:14:25.069 --> 00:14:26.538
in any of the "Matrix" movies.

00:14:26.538 --> 00:14:28.376
Niko: This is a film-history shot here.

00:14:28.376 --> 00:14:30.184
Wren: And it's like, you
can tell that they're CG.

00:14:30.184 --> 00:14:33.073
Here, I think the thing that
kind of fails a little bit

00:14:33.073 --> 00:14:35.512
is just the age of the rendering engine.

00:14:35.512 --> 00:14:36.936
It's the rendering of the skin

00:14:36.936 --> 00:14:38.552
that's really kind of hard to master.

00:14:38.552 --> 00:14:41.207
Clint: It kind of has, like,
a clay look to it almost.

00:14:41.207 --> 00:14:42.749
Wren: Yeah. You know, it's kind of lacking

00:14:42.749 --> 00:14:44.477
a little bit of that
subsurface scattering.

00:14:44.477 --> 00:14:46.406
Niko: The water droplets
are a little low-detail.

00:14:46.406 --> 00:14:47.473
Wren: They're, like, this long,

00:14:47.473 --> 00:14:48.833
and that's not how waterdrops look.

00:14:48.833 --> 00:14:49.988
They have a teardrop shape.

00:14:49.988 --> 00:14:52.180
Niko: Surface tension keeps the water

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:53.935
from being able to hold shapes like that.

00:14:53.935 --> 00:14:57.718
Wren: Yeah. But when
you do rain in a shot,

00:14:57.718 --> 00:14:59.731
you have motion blur, so
the rain always comes out

00:14:59.731 --> 00:15:02.284
as, like, these long, white strips.

00:15:02.284 --> 00:15:04.031
And I think it was probably just --

00:15:04.031 --> 00:15:05.006
Clint: A lot cooler if they're just

00:15:05.006 --> 00:15:07.051
these chunky, long water drops

00:15:07.051 --> 00:15:08.688
as opposed to these little tiny things.

00:15:08.688 --> 00:15:09.696
Wren: 'Cause there's reality,

00:15:09.696 --> 00:15:11.150
and then there's Hollywood reality.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:12.916
And Hollywood reality is basically just

00:15:12.916 --> 00:15:15.083
what you expect something to look like.

00:15:20.183 --> 00:15:22.420
The reason why I feel like a
shot like this doesn't work

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:24.441
is because he's punching him so hard,

00:15:24.441 --> 00:15:26.976
he is flying maybe 20 feet backwards

00:15:26.976 --> 00:15:28.599
and colliding with a rock wall

00:15:28.599 --> 00:15:30.531
hard enough to make it shatter.

00:15:30.531 --> 00:15:32.077
If that was to actually have

00:15:32.077 --> 00:15:34.326
the sort of impact to be able to do that,

00:15:34.326 --> 00:15:36.309
he'd be there in less than a single frame.

00:15:36.309 --> 00:15:38.636
He'd be like, kaboom!

00:15:38.636 --> 00:15:41.394
In this shot, it takes maybe
24 frames for him to go there.

00:15:41.394 --> 00:15:43.264
Clint: Also, you can see him
go like, [mimics movement].

00:15:43.264 --> 00:15:44.585
Wren: And that's the other
thing, is that, like,

00:15:44.585 --> 00:15:46.188
he actually has some up and down motion.

00:15:46.188 --> 00:15:47.295
Like, [mimics movement].

00:15:47.295 --> 00:15:48.586
It doesn't really work.

00:15:48.586 --> 00:15:49.806
Niko: So, what's most likely happening

00:15:49.806 --> 00:15:51.552
is there's a stuntman here that's on wires

00:15:51.552 --> 00:15:55.110
flying back into some sort of
pad or something like that,

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:57.061
meaning they had to cover up that pad.

00:15:57.061 --> 00:15:58.222
And if you look in the background,

00:15:58.222 --> 00:15:59.726
if you look at the bottom, there's,

00:15:59.726 --> 00:16:00.705
it almost looks like a face.

00:16:00.705 --> 00:16:01.977
You see, like, the two rocks --

00:16:01.977 --> 00:16:03.095
Wren: It's a repeated pattern!

00:16:03.095 --> 00:16:04.335
I see it, I totally see it!

00:16:04.335 --> 00:16:05.573
Niko: Some artist has gone in

00:16:05.573 --> 00:16:07.444
and taken a patch of the footage

00:16:07.444 --> 00:16:09.444
and duplicated it off this side.

00:16:09.444 --> 00:16:10.893
Wren: Yeah. But this happens so quickly.

00:16:10.893 --> 00:16:11.726
You're like, you can't tell.

00:16:11.726 --> 00:16:12.620
Clint: Yeah, there's no way.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:13.557
Yeah, could you imagine being in theaters

00:16:13.557 --> 00:16:15.753
and being like, "Wait a second! Hold on!

00:16:15.753 --> 00:16:17.638
Friggin' they clone-stamped it!"

00:16:17.638 --> 00:16:18.646
[all laughing]

00:16:18.646 --> 00:16:21.979
[Agent Smith crackling]

00:16:24.051 --> 00:16:25.720
Clint: All right, so
this is the final fight

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:26.774
from "Justice League."

00:16:26.774 --> 00:16:28.273
Let's take a look and see what they got.

00:16:28.273 --> 00:16:29.312
Full CG'd Batman.

00:16:29.312 --> 00:16:31.102
Wren: BatAffleck.

00:16:31.102 --> 00:16:32.777
Batfleck. Baffleck.
[crickets chirping]

00:16:33.859 --> 00:16:35.052
Clint: Full CG.

00:16:35.052 --> 00:16:36.417
It's, like, so much CG.

00:16:36.417 --> 00:16:37.625
Wren: So....

00:16:38.612 --> 00:16:39.929
Clint: It's -- oh, man.

00:16:41.876 --> 00:16:44.199
Wren: It's interesting, 'cause it....

00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:46.891
Clint: If everything was
just full CG, it'd be great.

00:16:46.891 --> 00:16:48.924
But when I see a real person,
it takes me out of it.

00:16:48.924 --> 00:16:51.231
Like, "Oh, that's one big green screen."

00:16:51.231 --> 00:16:52.770
Niko: Yeah, it's definitely
hitting that whole, like,

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:54.018
we shot this with a real camera,

00:16:54.018 --> 00:16:55.453
but everything is fake at this point

00:16:55.453 --> 00:16:57.803
except for, like, the
one actor in the shot.

00:16:57.803 --> 00:16:59.766
[all laughing]

00:16:59.766 --> 00:17:01.221
Clint: They just look so goofy, dude.

00:17:01.221 --> 00:17:02.827
There's a weird blend of, like --

00:17:02.827 --> 00:17:04.834
Wren: It's 'cause his
lip doesn't move at all.

00:17:04.834 --> 00:17:06.567
It's like -- like, put your lip here,

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:08.826
and it's like, "I prefer justice."

00:17:08.826 --> 00:17:10.411
[all laughing]

00:17:10.411 --> 00:17:13.351
And, like, that's the
very famous reason for it.

00:17:13.351 --> 00:17:15.618
Studios fighting over, like --

00:17:15.618 --> 00:17:17.001
Clint: Mustache contracts?

00:17:17.001 --> 00:17:18.859
Wren: It's a thing. They
had to deal with it.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:20.409
But everything we're seeing here

00:17:20.409 --> 00:17:22.695
is computer-generated.

00:17:22.695 --> 00:17:23.910
Like, literally everything.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:25.934
They're completely shot
on a green-screen stage,

00:17:25.934 --> 00:17:27.365
and so there's a disconnect.

00:17:27.365 --> 00:17:29.349
So, when, like, Cyborg
and Steppenwolf land here,

00:17:29.349 --> 00:17:31.320
this is all still 100% CG.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:32.236
Niko: Including Cyborg.

00:17:32.236 --> 00:17:33.344
Wren: Including Cyborg, exactly.

00:17:33.344 --> 00:17:34.250
Niko: You can tell by the motion.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:35.471
Clint: Yeah, when he looks over.

00:17:35.471 --> 00:17:38.540
It's very, like, [mimics
motion sound]. You know?

00:17:38.540 --> 00:17:39.900
Wren: But then it looks at these two kids

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:40.876
in the back of a pickup truck,

00:17:40.876 --> 00:17:43.012
and it's like, wow, that's obviously real.

00:17:43.012 --> 00:17:44.577
But everything else around is not.

00:17:44.577 --> 00:17:46.777
It's confusing our brains subconsciously.

00:17:46.777 --> 00:17:49.001
Looking at this slo-mo
punch scene, it's just...

00:17:49.001 --> 00:17:50.973
the beginning of it kind of confuses me,

00:17:50.973 --> 00:17:54.754
because Superman comes from
frame left into the shot,

00:17:54.754 --> 00:17:57.585
and I couldn't tell if he
was CG or not right away.

00:17:57.585 --> 00:18:01.202
There's something about the
look of his hair and ear

00:18:01.202 --> 00:18:03.297
that just looks CG to me at first.

00:18:03.297 --> 00:18:05.041
Like, even right here.

00:18:05.041 --> 00:18:06.360
Niko: Well, there's no shadow
of the fist on his face.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.037
Clint: It's like the
matte-clay kind of effect.

00:18:08.037 --> 00:18:11.418
Niko: Look at how dark the
ring finger is, of Steppenwolf,

00:18:11.418 --> 00:18:12.743
when it's by Henry Cavill's face.

00:18:12.743 --> 00:18:14.241
Right when it's brushing his nose.

00:18:14.241 --> 00:18:16.779
That's how dark the bottom of
Henry Cavill's nose should be.

00:18:16.779 --> 00:18:18.046
That's how dark the front
of his lips should be.

00:18:18.046 --> 00:18:18.956
Wren: I mean, you're totally right.

00:18:18.956 --> 00:18:20.868
Niko: They're all occupying
the same space right there.

00:18:20.868 --> 00:18:22.333
And then, just immediately,
your brain goes,

00:18:22.333 --> 00:18:23.543
"Yeah, something's weird."

00:18:23.543 --> 00:18:25.194
Clint: The gag is that
we got really close,

00:18:25.194 --> 00:18:26.705
and he nicked him, barely.

00:18:26.705 --> 00:18:28.943
And the shadow would bring his face

00:18:28.943 --> 00:18:32.113
and this fist visually closer together.

00:18:32.113 --> 00:18:33.628
Niko: Dude, compared
to the "Matrix" punch.

00:18:33.628 --> 00:18:35.755
This whole sequence really speaks to

00:18:35.755 --> 00:18:37.571
one of the challenges in the VFX industry.

00:18:37.571 --> 00:18:40.688
The level of rendering,
like, it's so high quality.

00:18:40.688 --> 00:18:42.340
The people that are doing
this are so good at it.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:44.795
But the problem is they're
working with footage

00:18:44.795 --> 00:18:47.196
that they had, like, five
minutes on set to grab,

00:18:47.196 --> 00:18:49.929
or, you know, a script that
never quite got finished.

00:18:49.929 --> 00:18:50.875
And they're doing their best,

00:18:50.875 --> 00:18:52.434
but you just get this really weird, like,

00:18:52.434 --> 00:18:54.505
why is my car painted in gold

00:18:54.505 --> 00:18:57.743
but there's, like, a
V-4 engine in the car?

00:18:57.743 --> 00:18:59.201
Clint: For sure. Yeah.
Niko: You know, it's like....

00:18:59.201 --> 00:19:00.659
Clint: It's the polished-turd
thing, is what you're saying.

00:19:00.659 --> 00:19:02.165
Niko: Yeah, it's the polished-turd thing.

00:19:02.165 --> 00:19:03.049
I hate to say it.

00:19:03.049 --> 00:19:04.276
And it's such a weird imbalance,

00:19:04.276 --> 00:19:06.869
and it kind of speaks to the
weird nature of filmmaking.

00:19:06.869 --> 00:19:08.333
What are you laughing at?

00:19:08.333 --> 00:19:09.862
Wren: Batman!

00:19:09.862 --> 00:19:11.003
[weapon pews]

00:19:12.593 --> 00:19:13.911
Clint: He was like, [grunts].

00:19:13.911 --> 00:19:16.604
And this little, like,
energy was like, pew!

00:19:16.604 --> 00:19:18.485
Wren: It's such a jarring cut.

00:19:18.485 --> 00:19:19.674
'Cause it's like, you
have these superheroes

00:19:19.674 --> 00:19:21.551
doing incredibly cool
stuff, and then it's like,

00:19:21.551 --> 00:19:23.476
oh, yeah, Batman's here,
and he's just like....

00:19:24.413 --> 00:19:27.333
The visual effects are
weak in certain areas

00:19:27.333 --> 00:19:28.512
but very strong in others.

00:19:28.512 --> 00:19:31.495
Like, the whole bridge-collapse
moment is great.

00:19:31.495 --> 00:19:33.662
The dust flying out, everything crunching

00:19:33.662 --> 00:19:35.427
and crumbling and moving around.

00:19:35.427 --> 00:19:36.827
When Cyborg here gets lifted up,

00:19:36.827 --> 00:19:38.720
I do like the rendering of this shot.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:40.429
Clint: Yeah, yeah, yeah.
It looks super cool.

00:19:40.429 --> 00:19:41.655
When he gets, like, torn in half.

00:19:41.655 --> 00:19:42.488
Wren: Yeah, exactly.

00:19:42.488 --> 00:19:44.442
And the light reflecting
up from down below,

00:19:44.442 --> 00:19:46.222
like, it looks very good.

00:19:46.222 --> 00:19:48.247
Steppenwolf: Now do you see it?

00:19:49.273 --> 00:19:51.622
[helicopter blades whirring]

00:19:51.622 --> 00:19:52.714
Wren: I think that's a real shot.

00:19:52.714 --> 00:19:53.549
Niko: I think so too.

00:19:53.549 --> 00:19:55.936
Clint: So, first off, that is incredible.

00:19:55.936 --> 00:19:57.993
You know, that's just two dudes.

00:19:57.993 --> 00:19:59.883
Wren: And they specifically
put stuff in the foreground

00:19:59.883 --> 00:20:01.909
to kind of show you that they're
shooting from the ground.

00:20:01.909 --> 00:20:04.187
All right. So, the crazy
thing about this scene

00:20:04.187 --> 00:20:07.031
is how much of it was actually real.

00:20:07.031 --> 00:20:10.522
They had 1,500 extras in this plaza,

00:20:10.522 --> 00:20:13.223
and they had two
helicopters flying around.

00:20:13.223 --> 00:20:15.888
One was a stunt helicopter,

00:20:15.888 --> 00:20:18.675
which, actually, the main
helicopter you're seeing,

00:20:18.675 --> 00:20:20.666
it is the Red Bull helicopter.

00:20:20.666 --> 00:20:21.499
Niko: Oh, really?

00:20:21.499 --> 00:20:22.983
I have seen the Red Bull helicopter.

00:20:22.983 --> 00:20:24.320
Wren: Yeah. With a paint job that actually

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.709
makes it look like a military helicopter.

00:20:26.709 --> 00:20:29.868
The big asterisk here is
that all of those stunts,

00:20:29.868 --> 00:20:32.472
with the helicopter doing
barrel rolls and flips,

00:20:32.472 --> 00:20:33.477
were not shot here.

00:20:33.477 --> 00:20:35.893
So, basically, everything I'm
gonna say from here on out

00:20:35.893 --> 00:20:37.422
is kind of speculation.

00:20:37.422 --> 00:20:39.993
But they literally physically
could not do that there,

00:20:39.993 --> 00:20:41.506
because this is Mexico City,

00:20:41.506 --> 00:20:44.519
with an elevation of 7,500 feet.

00:20:44.519 --> 00:20:46.744
Helicopters can obviously
fly around in Mexico City,

00:20:46.744 --> 00:20:48.802
but they can't do these sort of stunts,

00:20:48.802 --> 00:20:50.226
because the air is too thin

00:20:50.226 --> 00:20:51.376
and they don't have the force

00:20:51.376 --> 00:20:53.375
to be able to come out of a barrel roll.

00:20:53.375 --> 00:20:55.255
So they actually had to
film those barrel rolls

00:20:55.255 --> 00:20:57.010
and flips and stuff somewhere else

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:58.908
and CG in the city.

00:20:58.908 --> 00:21:00.011
Niko: So, the helicopter's real,

00:21:00.011 --> 00:21:01.719
but the entire background's fake?

00:21:01.719 --> 00:21:02.552
Wren: Yes.

00:21:02.552 --> 00:21:05.982
[dramatic music]
[helicopter blades whirring]

00:21:05.982 --> 00:21:07.127
The background is fake,

00:21:07.127 --> 00:21:09.431
but not in the CGI sort of fake.

00:21:09.431 --> 00:21:11.170
It's probably real plate photography

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:12.870
that they captured on set

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:15.009
and they're just animating
in the background.

00:21:15.009 --> 00:21:17.715
Niko: The quickest path to
realism in visual effects

00:21:17.715 --> 00:21:19.920
is to use all real
elements for your shots.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.321
If you have a real helicopter

00:21:21.321 --> 00:21:23.171
and a real picture of Mexico City

00:21:23.171 --> 00:21:25.051
and you stick them on top of each other,

00:21:25.051 --> 00:21:26.712
the shot's gonna look real.

00:21:26.712 --> 00:21:28.466
You know, at least most of it.

00:21:28.466 --> 00:21:30.873
Wren: But there are shots
where you can clearly see

00:21:30.873 --> 00:21:32.642
the helicopter doing a barrel roll

00:21:32.642 --> 00:21:34.742
above a bunch of people.

00:21:34.742 --> 00:21:36.411
No matter how safe you're being,

00:21:36.411 --> 00:21:38.094
that is still irresponsibly dangerous.

00:21:38.094 --> 00:21:41.430
And so for that, they
use crowd simulations,

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.348
where they actually CG in tons of people.

00:21:43.348 --> 00:21:44.507
And in some of these wide shots,

00:21:44.507 --> 00:21:46.152
you can kind of see it,
'cause you kind of see

00:21:46.152 --> 00:21:47.802
the running animation
in some of the people

00:21:47.802 --> 00:21:49.090
when they're really small, running away.

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:50.346
Clint: Like little RollerCoaster Tycoon

00:21:50.346 --> 00:21:51.692
animation people.

00:21:51.692 --> 00:21:52.525
Wren: Yeah.

00:21:52.525 --> 00:21:55.327
[dramatic music]

00:21:55.327 --> 00:21:57.337
Wren: They actually built
a separate helicopter

00:21:57.337 --> 00:22:00.943
on a rig, on a different
set, on hydraulics,

00:22:00.943 --> 00:22:03.443
that could actually, like,
move around like this.

00:22:03.443 --> 00:22:05.266
Clint: So it's like a fun Universal ride.

00:22:05.266 --> 00:22:07.791
Wren: Exactly. And so that's
all practical in that sense.

00:22:07.791 --> 00:22:09.427
We are dealing with green screen.

00:22:09.427 --> 00:22:10.926
Niko: That stuff screams CG.

00:22:10.926 --> 00:22:11.759
Wren: It's the close-ups,

00:22:11.759 --> 00:22:13.656
the external close-ups.
Niko: Oof, it's rough.

00:22:13.656 --> 00:22:14.653
Wren: It's the weakest point, for sure.

00:22:14.653 --> 00:22:15.835
Clint: The rest was just fantastic. Yeah.

00:22:15.835 --> 00:22:17.603
Niko: It has the same exact problem

00:22:17.603 --> 00:22:19.394
that Wolverine on the helicopter had,

00:22:19.394 --> 00:22:21.451
which is the helicopter's spinning,

00:22:21.451 --> 00:22:22.762
but the sunlight is not.

00:22:22.762 --> 00:22:24.044
Wren: That's the key thing.
Niko: Look at his face.

00:22:24.044 --> 00:22:26.481
He's lit from the left
side the entire time,

00:22:26.481 --> 00:22:28.136
even though the helicopter's spinning.

00:22:28.136 --> 00:22:30.398
Like, they should have thrown
a big old light on a stick

00:22:30.398 --> 00:22:32.042
and spun it around the set.

00:22:32.042 --> 00:22:33.708
In the outside shot,
there's no shadow on them

00:22:33.708 --> 00:22:34.861
whatsoever from the rotor,

00:22:34.861 --> 00:22:36.039
'cause the rotor is spinning so fast

00:22:36.039 --> 00:22:39.755
and becomes so thin that
it casts barely any shadow.

00:22:39.755 --> 00:22:40.693
But here in this close-up shot,

00:22:40.693 --> 00:22:42.034
it's just like, on, off; on, off; on, off.

00:22:42.034 --> 00:22:42.867
Really intense.

00:22:42.867 --> 00:22:44.584
Wren: You know what? I wonder if, maybe,

00:22:44.584 --> 00:22:45.951
they didn't have it to begin with.

00:22:45.951 --> 00:22:48.526
They could see the background,
it was clearly comped,

00:22:48.526 --> 00:22:50.827
and they were like, "How
can we make this better?"

00:22:50.827 --> 00:22:52.828
And they were like, "Well,
maybe we can add this flickering

00:22:52.828 --> 00:22:54.168
and it'll make it a
little bit more hectic."

00:22:54.168 --> 00:22:56.635
Niko: That's true. Because a
big chunk of visual effects

00:22:56.635 --> 00:22:58.009
is the sleight of hand.

00:22:58.009 --> 00:22:59.104
It's the little bit of camera shake,

00:22:59.104 --> 00:22:59.937
the little bit of blur,

00:22:59.937 --> 00:23:01.771
the little bit of, like,
particles in the air

00:23:01.771 --> 00:23:04.081
that you add over things
to kind of distract the eye

00:23:04.081 --> 00:23:05.993
that really makes things
look real sometimes.

00:23:05.993 --> 00:23:07.489
Clint: But it's this wide shot here

00:23:07.489 --> 00:23:09.449
where you can clearly see Daniel Craig.

00:23:09.449 --> 00:23:10.907
And I'm wondering, is this,

00:23:10.907 --> 00:23:12.277
are they actually doing this,

00:23:12.277 --> 00:23:14.072
and then it's a face replacement?

00:23:14.072 --> 00:23:15.063
Niko: They did the shadow thing!

00:23:15.063 --> 00:23:15.963
Wren: It's a head replacement!

00:23:15.963 --> 00:23:17.408
Niko: They did the
shadow thing to the head.

00:23:17.408 --> 00:23:18.398
Wren: They did do the head.

00:23:18.398 --> 00:23:19.231
Just kind of look at, like,

00:23:19.231 --> 00:23:21.112
the sharpness around his hair

00:23:21.112 --> 00:23:22.394
and kind of the brightness of it

00:23:22.394 --> 00:23:23.755
compared to the rest of his body.

00:23:23.755 --> 00:23:26.383
So, it's a stunt guy doing this.

00:23:26.383 --> 00:23:28.082
We know that is definitely
not Daniel Craig

00:23:28.082 --> 00:23:30.312
hanging outside this
real flying helicopter.

00:23:30.312 --> 00:23:31.752
Clint: Just like Hugo Weaving,

00:23:31.752 --> 00:23:33.899
they have a full photo scan
of Daniel Craig's face,

00:23:33.899 --> 00:23:36.364
and they just put it on there.

00:23:36.364 --> 00:23:37.197
Is that a flare gun?

00:23:37.197 --> 00:23:38.030
Wren: Yeah, that was a flare gun.

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.217
Niko: I love that stuff,
with the gravity shifting.

00:23:40.217 --> 00:23:42.251
Clint: Yeah. You can actually tell

00:23:42.251 --> 00:23:44.916
that it was shifting for
real, for those actors.

00:23:44.916 --> 00:23:46.450
Niko: Yeah. They're holding on for real,

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:47.674
as, like, something spins,

00:23:47.674 --> 00:23:49.357
the set spins or something. That's cool.

00:23:49.357 --> 00:23:50.904
Wren: So, another thing to note is that

00:23:50.904 --> 00:23:52.428
you see people hanging
out of the helicopter

00:23:52.428 --> 00:23:53.876
when it's doing the barrel roll.

00:23:53.876 --> 00:23:54.709
They're mannequins.

00:23:54.709 --> 00:23:56.190
And you can clearly see, like,

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:57.508
this dude just kind of hanging out,

00:23:57.508 --> 00:23:58.716
kind of stuck there.

00:23:58.716 --> 00:24:00.600
Niko: Are you sure they're mannequins?

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:01.976
I think that's a real person.

00:24:01.976 --> 00:24:04.513
Wren: Niko, I honestly, 100% guarantee you

00:24:04.513 --> 00:24:06.071
that is not a real person.

00:24:08.667 --> 00:24:09.789
Niko: He moves his hand right there!

00:24:09.789 --> 00:24:11.019
He's a human being.

00:24:11.019 --> 00:24:12.269
Producer: That looks CG.

00:24:12.269 --> 00:24:13.704
Niko: Could be. He could be CG.

00:24:13.704 --> 00:24:14.642
Wren: We're forgetting
something very important

00:24:14.642 --> 00:24:15.475
about that shot.

00:24:15.475 --> 00:24:17.106
The entire background isn't real.

00:24:17.106 --> 00:24:19.006
Niko: You're right.
That guy's probably CG.

00:24:19.006 --> 00:24:21.099
Clint: I think the
camera shake they added.

00:24:21.099 --> 00:24:23.667
Like, that shot specifically is, like,

00:24:23.667 --> 00:24:26.451
2D, post camera shake.

00:24:26.451 --> 00:24:28.393
Wren: Whereas if you compare
it to "Mission: Impossible,"

00:24:28.393 --> 00:24:30.195
where there were definitely several shots

00:24:30.195 --> 00:24:31.550
where Tom Cruise is legitimately

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:33.250
flying this helicopter, doing stunts,

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:35.296
you can kind of see micro-vibrations

00:24:35.296 --> 00:24:37.948
of the flexing of the rig.

00:24:37.948 --> 00:24:39.223
Whereas here, it's like the camera's

00:24:39.223 --> 00:24:40.414
actually moving up and down,

00:24:40.414 --> 00:24:41.935
because this is just a 2D cutout

00:24:41.935 --> 00:24:43.583
of the image going up and down.

00:24:43.583 --> 00:24:45.327
Niko: They're giving you car-camera shake.

00:24:45.327 --> 00:24:46.754
You're talking about
how the background's CG.

00:24:46.754 --> 00:24:49.316
Notice the bright daylight

00:24:49.316 --> 00:24:51.436
on the front of the
helicopter glass standing out.

00:24:51.436 --> 00:24:53.018
I think that's the actual sky

00:24:53.018 --> 00:24:53.951
and the actual daylight

00:24:53.951 --> 00:24:54.956
and the actual background

00:24:54.956 --> 00:24:56.189
from where they filmed the helicopter

00:24:56.189 --> 00:24:57.191
in a different spot.

00:24:57.191 --> 00:25:00.018
And so you get a little
bit of that sense of, like,

00:25:00.018 --> 00:25:01.468
huh, that light's coming from a thing

00:25:01.468 --> 00:25:03.132
that doesn't quite exist in this scene.

00:25:03.132 --> 00:25:04.604
Wren: I find it amusing that, like,

00:25:04.604 --> 00:25:07.372
this helicopter just
keeps doing stunt moves.

00:25:07.372 --> 00:25:08.654
It keeps doing barrels.

00:25:08.654 --> 00:25:10.292
And now it's doing this crazy flip.

00:25:10.292 --> 00:25:11.829
Clint: Oh, here it comes! Zero g's!

00:25:11.829 --> 00:25:12.914
Yeah, that's pretty sweet.

00:25:12.914 --> 00:25:14.288
Niko: This is a cool
idea for a fight scene.

00:25:14.288 --> 00:25:15.121
Clint: Yeah.

00:25:15.121 --> 00:25:16.771
He's so calm and collected.

00:25:20.842 --> 00:25:22.597
Wren: Man, that was wild.

00:25:22.597 --> 00:25:24.591
I feel like I actually learned quite a bit

00:25:24.591 --> 00:25:26.516
from this episode. More
than I anticipated.

00:25:26.516 --> 00:25:27.349
Niko: If you guys enjoyed this,

00:25:27.349 --> 00:25:29.574
maybe consider checking out
our channel, Corridor Crew,

00:25:29.574 --> 00:25:31.622
and watching the "VFX Artists React" show.

00:25:31.622 --> 00:25:32.835
To all of you watching,

00:25:32.835 --> 00:25:34.251
we love making this show.

00:25:34.251 --> 00:25:36.363
Thank you so much for
watching and supporting it.

00:25:36.363 --> 00:25:40.399
We are going to continue making
VFX shows forever. [laughs]

